  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers and thunderstorms may continue to affect our southern \nmost zones in Delmarva this evening but the threat for severe \nweather and significant flash flooding is low. \n\nAs of early this evening, a cold front extends from near coastal \nMonmouth County south and west across south Jersey and then across \ncentral Delmarva. North of this boundary, low level cold air \nadvection is underway with falling dew points. This is helping to \nstabilize things so no showers/storms are expected from this point \nforward for the majority of the area. South of the front it's still \na bit more interesting as showers and storms are continuing to \naffect southern portions of eastern Maryland into Sussex County \nDelaware. Fortunately, recent radar trends show a weakening of \nthese storms and the other good news is that these storms have \nbeen affecting areas just south of where the very heavy rainfall\namounts occurred last night in northern Sussex County. That \nsaid, we'll still have to watch this area through at least this \nevening as front is slowing down as it pushes into this area \nwith some weak impulses aloft moving through in the upper level \nflow. The severe weather threat appears low at this time with a \nsomewhat greater potential though for localized flooding over \nportions of Sussex County DE. The front should finally be \noffshore bringing any showers/storms to an end by morning. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Fair and dry weather is expected for Thursday right through most \nof the weekend. \n\nHigh pressure will build in for Thursday and continue to dominate \nthe area through Saturday before starting to move offshore Sunday. \nThis will bring several days of sunshine, comfortable humidity \nlevels, and temperatures a bit below average for this time of\nyear. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A pair of systems will bring more thunderstorm \nchances early next week.\n\nAn open wave, mid level, short wave trough will be crossing \nthrough the Northeast on Sunday. Currently, we have relatively \nlow chances for precipitation with this trough, primarily \nbecause there is limited opportunity for moisture advection \nbefore this trough arrives. Additionally, at this point there \ndoesn't appear to be much in the way of boundary layer\nconvergence.\n\nThere is a different story with the next mid/upper level trough\napproaching our region Tuesday into Wednesday. For this system,\nthere will likely be ample opportunity for moisture advection\nMonday into Tuesday. Additionally, it is likely to be\naccompanied by a low and at least a cold front at the surface.\nThat being said, there is considerable differences between\nmodels on how this system will evolve. Some models are depicting\nthis as an open wave, fast moving trough with only a limited\nopportunity for rain. Others, most notably the ECMWF, depict the\nmid upper level trough becoming a closed low, and then slowing\nits eastward progress considerably. Given the large differences\nin uncertainty, forecast stays close to the blend of guidance\nthrough this period.",
